Why is a tree appropriate for the doctor structure?

I think a binary tree is a good choice for organizing the doctor reporting structure because it clearly shows the relationships between doctors.
Each doctor has a specific position in the tree, and the root represents the doctor at the top of the hierarchy.
For example, in our assignment, Dr. Croft is the root, while Dr. Phan and Dr. Goldsmith report directly to Dr. Croft. 
I think this structure makes it easier to understand how the hospital's reporting system is organized.

When might a software engineer use preorder, inorder, or postorder traversals?

While working on this assignment, I learned that software engineers use different tree traversal methods depending on the task. 
Preorder traversal processes the root before its children, making it useful when copying a tree. 
Inorder traversal visits the left subtree, then the root, and finally the right subtree. 
This method is helpful when working with a binary search tree because it returns values in sorted order. 
Postorder traversal processes the children before the parent, making it useful when deleting a tree or freeing up memory. 
These examples helped me understand why choosing the right traversal method matters in programming.

How do heaps help simulate real-time systems like emergency intake?

I think a min-heap is useful for emergency intake because it helps hospitals treat patients based on their urgency rather than their arrival order.
In our program, each patient has an urgency score from 1 to 10, where 1 represents the highest priority. 
For example, Taylor has an urgency of 1, so Taylor should be treated before Jordan, who has an urgency of 3. 
When a new patient is added, the heapify_up() method helps move them to the correct position based on their urgency. 
When the most urgent patient is removed using remove_min(), heapify_down() reorganizes the remaining patients to maintain the heap structure. 
I learned that a heap makes it possible to quickly access the most urgent patient without searching through the entire list each time. 
This makes it a good choice for systems where priorities can change as new patients arrive.